ientific literature. In\nL891@P10: Proc. ACM Int. Conference on Information & Knowl\u0002edge Management. ACM, pages 1733–1738.\nL892@P10: Adam Vogel and Dan Jurafsky. 2012. He said, she said:\nL893@P10: Gender in the ACL anthology. In Proc. ACL Special\nL894@P10: Workshop on Rediscovering 50 Years of Discoveries.\nL895@P10: pages 33–41.\nL896@P10: Sam Wiseman, Alexander M. Rush, and Stuart M.\nL897@P10: Shieber. 2016. Learning global features for coref\u0002erence resolution. In HLT-NAACL.\nL898@P10: Yan Xu, Ran Jia, Lili Mou, Ge Li, Yunchuan Chen,\nL899@P10: Yangyang Lu, and Zhi Jin. 2016. Improved rela\u0002tion classification by deep recurrent neural networks\nL900@P10: with data augmentation. In Proc. Int. Conf. Compu\u0002tational Linguistics (COLING). pages 1461–1470.\nL901@P10: Congle Zhang, Stephen Soderland, and Daniel S. Weld.\nL902@P10: 2015. Exploiting parallel news streams for unsuper\u0002vised event extraction. TACL 3:117–129.\nL903@P10: Meishan Zhang, Yue Zhang, and Guohong Fu. 2017.\nL904@P10: End-to-end neural relation extraction with global op\u0002timization. In Proc. Conf. Empirical Methods Natu\u0002ral Language Process. (EMNLP). pages 1730–1740.\nL905@P10: Suncong Zheng, Feng Wang, Hongyun Bao, Yuexing\nL906@P10: Hao, Peng Zhou, and Bo Xu. 2017. Joint extrac\u0002tion of entities and relations based on a novel tag\u0002ging scheme. In Proc. Annu. Meeting Assoc. for\nL907@P10: Computational Linguistics (ACL). volume 1, pages\nL908@P10-11: 1227–1236.3230\nL909@P11: A Annotation Guideline\nL910@P11: A.1 Entity Category\nL911@P11: • Task: Applications, problems to solve, sys\u0002tems to construct.\nL912@P11: E.g. information extraction, machine reading\nL913@P11: system, image segmentation, etc.\nL914@P11: • Method: Methods , models, systems to use,\nL915@P11: or tools, components of a system, frameworks.\nL916@P11: E.g. language model, CORENLP, POS parser,\nL917@P11: kernel method, etc.\nL918@P11: • Evaluation Metric: Metrics, measures, or\nL919@P11: entities that can express quality of a sys\u0002tem/method.\nL920@P11: E.g. F1, BLEU, Precision, Recall, ROC curve,\nL921@P11: mean reciprocal rank, mean-squared error, ro\u0002bustness, time complexity, etc.\nL922@P11: • Material: Data, datasets, resources, Corpus,\nL923@P11: Knowledge base.\nL924@P11: E.g. image data, speech data, stereo images,\nL925@P11: bilingual dictionary, paraphrased questions,\nL926@P11: CoNLL, Panntreebank, WordNet, Wikipedia,\nL927@P11: etc.\nL928@P11: • Evaluation Metric: Metric measure or term\nL929@P11: that can express quality of a system/method.\nL930@P11: E.g. F1, BLEU, Precision, Recall, ROC\nL931@P11: curve, mean reciprocal rank, mean-squared\nL932@P11: error,robustness, compile time, time complex\u0002ity...\nL933@P11: • Generic: General terms or pronouns that may\nL934@P11: refer to a entity but are not themselves infor\u0002mative, often used as connection words.\nL935@P11: E.g model, approach, prior knowledge, them,\nL936@P11: it...\nL937@P11: A.2 Relation Category\nL938@P11: Relation link can not go beyond sentence boundary.\nL939@P11: We define 4 asymmetric relation types (Used-for,\nL940@P11: Feature-of, Hyponym-of, Part-of), together with 2\nL941@P11: symmetric relation types (Compare, Conjunction).\nL942@P11: B always points to A for asymmetric relations\nL943@P11: • Used-for: B is used for A, B models A, A is\nL944@P11: trained on B, B exploits A, A is based on B.\nL945@P11: E.g.\nL946@P11: The TISPER system has been designed\nL947@P11: to enable many text applications.\nL948@P11: Our method models user proficiency.\nL949@P11: Our algorithms exploits local soothness.\nL950@P11: • Feature-of: B belongs to A, B is a feature of\nL951@P11: A, B is under A domain. E.g.\nL952@P11: prior knowledge of the model\nL953@P11: genre-specific regularities of discourse\nL954@P11: structure\nL955@P11: English text in science domain\nL956@P11: • Hyponym-of: B is a hyponym of A, B is a\nL957@P11: type of A. E.g.\nL958@P11: TUIT is a software library\nL959@P11: NLP applications such as machine trans\u0002lation and language generation\nL960@P11: • Part-of: B is a part of A... E.g.\nL961@P11: The system includes two models: speech\nL962@P11: recognition and natural language under\u0002standing\nL963@P11: We incorporate NLU module to the sys\u0002tem.\nL964@P11: • Compare: Symmetric relation (use blue to\nL965@P11: denote entity). Opposite of conjunction, com\u0002pare two models/methods, or listing two op\u0002posing entities. E.g.\nL966@P11: Unlike the quantitative prior, the qualita\u0002tive prior is often ignored...\nL967@P11: We compare our system with previous\nL968@P11: sequential tagging systems...\nL969@P11: • Conjunction: Symmetric relation (use blue\nL970@P11: to denote entity). Function as similar role or\nL971@P11: use/incorporate with. E.g.\nL972@P11: obtained from human expert or knowl\u0002edge base\nL973@P11: NLP applications such as machine trans\u0002lation and language generation\nL974@P11: A.3 Coreference\nL975@P11: Two Entities that points to the same concept.\nL976@P11: • Anaphora and Cataphora:\nL977@P11: We introduce a machine reading system...\nL978@P11: The system...\nL979@P11: The prior knowledge include...Such\nL980@P11: knowledge can be applied to...\nL981@P11: • Coreferring noun phrase:\nL982@P11-12: We develop a part-of-speech tagging sys\u0002tem...The POS tagger...3231\nL983@P12: A.4 Notes\nL984@P12: 1. Entity boundary annotation follows the\nL985@P12: ACL RD-TEC Annotation Guideline (Qasem\u0002iZadeh and Schumann, 2016), with the exten\u0002tion that spans can be embedded in longer\nL986@P12: spans, only if the shorter span is involved in a\nL987@P12: relation.\nL988@P12: 2. Do not include determinators (such as the, a),\nL989@P12: or adjective pronouns (such as this,its, these,\nL990@P12: such) to the span. If generic phrases are not\nL991@P12: involved in a relation, do not tag them.\nL992@P12: 3. Do not tag relation if one entity is:\nL993@P12: • Variable bound:\nL994@P12: We introduce a neural based approach..\nL995@P12: Its benefit is...\nL996@P12: • The word which:\nL997@P12: We introduce a neural based approach,\nL998@P12: which is a...\nL999@P12: 4. Do not tag coreference if the entity is\nL1000@P12: • Generically-used Other-ScientificTerm:\nL1001@P12: ...advantage gained from local smooth\u0002ness which... We present algorithms ex\u0002ploiting local smoothness in more aggres\u0002sive ways...\nL1002@P12: • Same scientific term but refer to different\nL1003@P12: examples:\nL1004@P12: We use a data structure, we also use an\u0002other data structure...\nL1005@P12: 5. Do not label negative relations:\nL1006@P12: X is not used in Y or X is hard to be applied\nL1007@P12: in Y\nL1008@P12: B Annotation and Knowledge Graph\nL1009@P12: Examples\nL1010@P12: Here we take a screen shot of the BRAT interface\nL1011@P12: for an ACL paper in Figure 9. We also attach the\nL1012@P12: original figure of Figure 3 in Figure 10. More\nL1013@P12: examples can be found in the project website4.\nL1014@P12-13: 4http://nlp.cs.washington.edu/sciIE/3232\nL1015@P13: Figure 9: Annotation example 1 from ACL\nL1016@P13: Figure 10: An example of our automatically generated knowledge graph centered on statistical machine\nL1017@P13: translation. This is the original figure of Figure 4.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMulti-Task Identification of Entities, Relations, and Coreference for Scientific Knowledge Graph Construction (https://aclanthology.org/D18-1360.pdf)\nciteturn28view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn27view1\",\"pattern\":\"greedy\"}); Total lines: 1018\nL98@P1: three types of entities (called keyphrases): Tasks,\nL99@P1: Methods, and Materials and two relation types:\nL100@P1: hyponym-of and synonym-of. SemEval 18 (Gabor ´\nL101@P1: et al., 2018) is focused on predicting relations be\u0002tween entities within a sentence. It consists of six\nL102@P1: relation types. Using these datasets, neural mod\u0002els (Ammar et al., 2017, 2018; Luan et al., 2017b;\nL103@P1: Augenstein and Søgaard, 2017) are introduced for\nL104@P1: extracting scientific information. We extend these\nL105@P1: datasets by increasing relation coverage, adding\nL106@P1: cross-sentence coreference linking, and removing\nL107@P1: some annotation constraints. Different from most\nL108@P1: previous IE systems for scientific literature and gen\u0002eral domains (Miwa and Bansal, 2016; Xu et al.,\nL109@P1: 2016; Peng et al., 2017; Quirk and Poon, 2017;\nL110@P1: Luan et al., 2018; Adel and Schutze ¨ , 2017), which\nL111@P1: use preprocessed syntactic, discourse or corefer\u0002ence features as input, our unified framework does\nL112@P1: not rely on any pipeline processing and is able to\nL113@P1: model overlapping spans.\nL114@P1: While Singh et al. (2013) show improvements\nL115@P1: by jointly modeling entities, relations, and coref\u0002erence links, most recent neural models for these\nL116@P1: tasks focus on single tasks (Clark and Manning,\nL117@P1: 2016; Wiseman et al., 2016; Lee et al., 2017; Lam\u0002ple et al., 2016; Peng et al., 2017) or joint entity\nL118@P1: and relation extraction (Katiyar and Cardie, 2017;\nL119@P1: Zhang et al., 2017; Adel and Schutze ¨ , 2017; Zheng\nL120@P1: et al., 2017). Among those studies, many papers as\u0002sume the entity boundaries are given, such as (Clark\nL121@P1: and Manning, 2016), Adel and Schutze ¨ (2017) and\nL122@P1: Peng et al. (2017). Our work relaxes this constraint\nL123@P1: and predicts entity boundaries by optimizing over\nL124@P1: all possible spans. Our model draws from recent\nL125@P1: end-to-end span-based models for coreference res\u0002olution (Lee et al., 2017, 2018) and semantic role\nL126@P1: labeling (He et al., 2018) and extends them for the\nL127@P1: multi-task framework involving the three tasks of\nL128@P1: identification of entity, relation and coreference.\nL129@P1: Neural multi-task learning has been applied to\nL130@P1: a range of NLP tasks. Most of these models share\nL131@P1: word-level representations (Collobert and Weston,\nL132@P1: 2008; Klerke et al., 2016; Luan et al., 2016, 2017a;\nL133@P1: Rei, 2017), while Peng et al. (2017) uses high-order\nL134@P1-2: cross-task factors. Our model instead propagates3221\nL135@P2: cross-task information via span representations,\nL136@P2: which is related to Swayamdipta et al. (2017).\nL137@P2: 3 Dataset\nL138@P2: Our dataset (called SCIERC) includes annotations\nL139@P2: for scientific entities, their relations, and corefer\u0002ence clusters for 500 scientific abstracts. These ab\u0002stracts are taken from 12 AI conference/workshop\nL140@P2: proceedings in four AI communities from the Se\u0002mantic Scholar Corpus2\nL141@P2: . SCIERC extends pre\u0002vious datasets in scientific articles SemEval 2017\nL142@P2: Task 10 (SemEval 17) (Augenstein et al., 2017) and\nL143@P2: SemEval 2018 Task 7 (SemEval 18) (Gabor et al. ´ ,\nL144@P2: 2018) by extending entity types, relation types, rela\u0002tion coverage, and adding cross-sentence relations\nL145@P2: using coreference links. Our dataset is publicly\nL146@P2: available at: http://nlp.cs.washington.\nL147@P2: edu/sciIE/. Table 1 shows the statistics of SCI\u0002ERC.\nL148@P2: Annotation Scheme We define six types for an\u0002notating scientific entities (Task, Method, Metric,\nL149@P2: Material, Other-ScientificTerm and Generic) and\nL150@P2: seven relation types (Compare, Part-of, Conjunc\u0002tion, Evaluate-for, Feature-of, Used-for, Hyponym\u0002Of). Directionality is taken into account except\nL151@P2: for the two symmetric relation types (Conjunction\nL152@P2: and Compare). Coreference links are annotated\nL153@P2: between identical scientific entities. A Generic en\u0002tity is annotated only when the entity is involved\nL154@P2: in a relation or is coreferred with another entity.\nL155@P2: Annotation guidelines can be found in Appendix A.\nL156@P2: Figure 1 shows an annotated example.\nL157@P2: Following annotation guidelines from Qasem\u0002iZadeh and Schumann (2016) and using the BRAT\nL158@P2: interface (Stenetorp et al., 2012), our annotators\nL159@P2: perform a greedy annotation for spans and always\nL160@P2: prefer the longer span whenever ambiguity occurs.\nL161@P2: Nested spans are allowed when a subspan has a\nL162@P2: relation/coreference link with another term outside\nL163@P2: the span.\nL164@P2: Human Agreements One domain expert anno\u0002tated all the documents in the dataset; 12% of the\nL165@P2: data is dually annotated by 4 other domain experts\nL166@P2: to evaluate the user agreements. The kappa score\nL167@P2: for annotating entities is 76.9%, relation extraction\nL168@P2: is 67.8% and coreference is 63.8%.\nL169@P2: 2These conferences include general AI (AAAI, IJCAI),\nL170@P2: NLP (ACL, EMNLP, IJCNLP), speech (ICASSP, Interspeech),\nL171@P2: machine learning (NIPS, ICML), and computer vision (CVPR,\nL172@P2: ICCV, ECCV) at http://labs.semanticscholar.\nL173@P2: org/corpus/\nL174@P2: Statistics SCIERC SemEval 17 SemEval 18\nL175@P2: #Entities 8089 9946 7483\nL176@P2: #Relations 4716 672 1595\nL177@P2: #Relations/Doc 9.4 1.3 3.2\nL178@P2: #Coref links 2752 - -\nL179@P2: #Coref clusters 1023 - -\nL180@P2: Table 1: Dataset statistics for our dataset SCIERC\nL181@P2: and two previous datasets on scientific information\nL182@P2: extraction. All datasets annotate 500 documents.\nL183@P2: Comparison with previous datasets SCIERC\nL184@P2: is focused on annotating cross-sentence relations\nL185@P2: and has more relation coverage than SemEval 17\nL186@P2: and SemEval 18, as shown in Table 1. SemEval 17\nL187@P2: is mostly designed for entity recognition and only\nL188@P2: covers two relation types. The task in SemEval 18\nL189@P2: is to classify a relation between a pair of entities\nL190@P2: given entity boundaries, but only intra-sentence re\u0002lations are annotated and each entity only appears\nL191@P2: in one relation, resulting in sparser relation cover\u0002age than our dataset (3.2 vs. 9.4 relations per ab\u0002stract). SCIERC extends these datasets by adding\nL192@P2: more relation types and coreference clusters, which\nL193@P2: allows representing cross-sentence relations, and\nL194@P2: removing annotation constraints. Table 1 gives a\nL195@P2: comparison of statistics among the three datasets.\nL196@P2: In addition, SCIERC aims at including broader\nL197@P2: coverage of general AI communities.\nL198@P2: 4 Model\nL199@P2: We develop a unified framework (called SCIIE)\nL200@P2: to identify and classify scientific entities, relations,\nL201@P2: and coreference resolution across sentences. SCIIE\nL202@P2: is a multi-task learning setup that extends previous\nL203@P2: span-based models for coreference resolution (Lee\nL204@P2: et al., 2017) and semantic role labeling (He et al.,\nL205@P2: 2018). All three tasks of entity recognition, re\u0002lation extraction, and coreference resolution are\nL206@P2: treated as multinomial classification problems with\nL207@P2: shared span representations. SCIIE benefits from\nL208@P2: expressive contextualized span representations as\nL209@P2: classifier features. By sharing span representations,\nL210@P2: sentence-level tasks can benefit from information\nL211@P2: propagated from coreference resolution across sen\u0002tences, without increasing the complexity of infer\u0002ence. Figure 2 shows a high-level overview of the\nL212@P2: SCIIE multi-task framework.\nL213@P2: 4.1 Problem Definition\nL214@P2: The input is a document represented as a sequence\nL215@P2-3: of words D = {w1, . . . , wn}, from which we de\u0002rive S = {s1, . . . , sN }, the set of all possible3222\nL216@P3: MORPA\nL217@P3: MORPA is a\nL218@P3: a fully implemented parser\nL219@P3: parser\nL220@P3: MORphological Parser MORPA\nL221@P3: …, the MORphological Parser MORPA is provided with a … MORPA is a fully implemented parser developed for …\nL222@P3: MORphological\nL223@P3: MORPA is\nL224@P3: is provided with\nL225@P3: NULL\nL226@P3: Hyponym-of Used-for NULL\nL227@P3: Task Method NULL\nL228@P3: Sentences\nL229@P3: BiLSTM outputs\nL230@P3: Span\nL231@P3: Representations\nL232@P3: Entity\nL233@P3: Recognition\nL234@P3: +Span Features\nL235@P3: Coreference\nL236@P3: Resolution\nL237@P3: Relation\nL238@P3: Extraction\nL239@P3: … …\nL240@P3: MORPA parser\nL241@P3: MORphological\nL242@P3: MORphological Parser MORPA\nL243@P3: …\nL244@P3: … …\nL245@P3: Figure 2: Overview of the multitask setup, where all three tasks are treated as classification problems on\nL246@P3: top of shared span representations. Dotted arcs indicate the normalization space for each task.\nL247@P3: within-sentence word sequence spans (up to a rea\u0002sonable length) in the document. The output con\u0002tains three structures: the entity types E for all\nL248@P3: spans S, the relations R for all pair of spans S × S,\nL249@P3: and the coreference links C for all spans in S. The\nL250@P3: output structures are represented with a set of dis\u0002crete random variables indexed by spans or pairs\nL251@P3: of spans. Specifically, the output structures are\nL252@P3: defined as follows.\nL253@P3: Entity recognition is to predict the best entity type\nL254@P3: for every candidate span. Let LE represent the set\nL255@P3: of all possible entity types including the null-type \u000f.\nL256@P3: The output structure E is a set of random variables\nL257@P3: indexed by spans: ei ∈ LE for i = 1, . . . , N.\nL258@P3: Relation extraction is to predict the best relation\nL259@P3: type given an ordered pair of spans (si, sj ). Let LR\nL260@P3: be the set of all possible relation types including\nL261@P3: the null-type \u000f. The output structure R is a set of\nL262@P3: random variables indexed over pairs of spans (i, j)\nL263@P3: that belong to the same sentence: rij ∈ LR for\nL264@P3: i, j = 1, . .